@gmarull 
I was not able to review your changes earlier. I see in that line can happen potential overflow. Variable `tmp` can have maximum value 130. That means equation `(tmp * 65535) / 100` result 85195 will be definitely bigger than `int16_t` of the `t_ticks` variable.

`t_ticks` is `int16_t`. Maximum is `#define __INT16_MAX__ 0x7fff` or `32767`

> Adding 0.5 and casting to `int` rounds to the nearest integer. Integer division truncates down.

That would be true if the first operand was a float. I've done a quick test just in case:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

int main(void)
{
	for (int32_t tmp = INT32_MIN; tmp < INT32_MAX; tmp++) {
		int16_t rh_ticks1;
		int16_t rh_ticks2;

		rh_ticks1 = (tmp * 0xFFFF / 100U) + 0.5;
		rh_ticks2 = (tmp * 0xFFFF / 100U);

		if (rh_ticks1 != rh_ticks2) {
			printf("ERROR! %d, %d\n", rh_ticks1, rh_ticks2);
			return 1;
		}
	}

	return 0;
}
```

Code never gets into the error branch. `rh_ticks1 = (int16_t)((float)(tmp * 0xFFFF / 100U) + 0.5);` would do what you mean I guess. Still not sure if it's the best approach, see https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~rbd/papers/cmj-float-to-int.html

> 
> > (Table 9)
> 
> I just looked at the datasheet and I think this is a typo; you mean table 10, right?

Right, my mistake.

> 
> > this rounding is not mentioned, this is why I ended up removing it.
> 
> The formula in table 10 looks like an abstract mathematical equation for converting a percentage from 0 to 100 to ticks from 0 to 65,535, where footnote 4 on page 3 says that ticks are proportional to the log of the resistance of the sensing layer. So this sure looks like an attempt to get the nearest integer to something that varies continuously.
> 
> Why not use the usual idiom for rounding to the nearest integer that stays in integers without requiring floating point?

Good point. Let me tag the original author, so he can decide. @leonardp. I should have added him to the review...

Hi!
I originally checked this part of the driver against the "Defaults" row in Table 10 of the datasheet.
Consider this test/example:
```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>

int main(void)
{
	uint16_t t_ticks1, t_ticks2;
	uint16_t rh_ticks1, rh_ticks2;

	float t_default = 25;
	float rh_default = 50;

	uint16_t t_ticks_default = 0x6666;
	uint16_t rh_ticks_default = 0x8000;

	t_ticks1 = ((t_default + 45U) * 0xFFFF / 175U) + 0.5;
	t_ticks2 = ((t_default + 45) * 65535) / 175;
	rh_ticks1 = (rh_default * 0xFFFF / 100U) + 0.5;
	rh_ticks2 = (rh_default * 65535) / 100;

	if ( t_ticks1 != t_ticks_default){
		printf("ERR(t_ticks1): %d != %d\n", t_ticks1, t_ticks_default);
	}
	if ( t_ticks2 != t_ticks_default){
		printf("ERR(t_ticks2): %d != %d\n", t_ticks2, t_ticks_default);
	}
	if ( rh_ticks1 != rh_ticks_default){
		printf("ERR(rh_ticks1): %d != %d\n", rh_ticks1, rh_ticks_default);
	}
	if ( rh_ticks2 != rh_ticks_default){
		printf("ERR(rh_ticks2): %d != %d\n", rh_ticks2, rh_ticks_default);
	}

	return 0;
}
```
This prints: `ERR(rh_ticks2): 32767 != 32768`.
Changing the type of the defaults from `float` to `int16_t`/`uint16_t` (later stages of the driver development) yields:
```
ERR(rh_ticks1): 32767 != 32768
ERR(rh_ticks2): 32767 != 32768
```
TL;DR: was meant to be done is this:
> @gmarull 
rh_ticks1 = (int16_t)((float)(tmp * 0xFFFF / 100U) + 0.5); would do what you mean I guess.

What was actually done is something different..

IMHO the most elegant way would be this:
> @mbolivar-nordic 
> ```c
> rh_ticks = ((tmp * 65535) + 50) / 100;
> ```
But then `t_ticks` should probably be changed to 
```C
t_ticks = ((tmp * 65535) + 87) / 175;
```
which I wouldn't hate.

I could make a pull request incorporating the approach from @mbolivar-nordic but I would also be fine with keeping the rounding errors.

For reference this is [Sensirion's approach](https://github.com/Sensirion/embedded-sgp/blob/master/sgp40/sgp40.c#L60-L83) which i would avoid for the sake of clarity.

